  </div>
</section>

<!-- Gender Disparity -->
<section id="sex-disparity">
  <h2>Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</h2>
  <p>Males are disproportionately represented among homicide victims. In 2023, the median male homicide rate across reporting countries was ''' + str(data['stats']['male_median_2023']) + ''' per 100,000—more than three times the female median of ''' + str(data['stats']['female_median_2023']) + '''. The male-to-female ratio (median across countries) stands at <strong>''' + str(data['stats']['male_female_ratio_median']) + '''×</strong>, a figure that has remained relatively stable over the past three decades, fluctuating between roughly 2.5× and 3.5×.</p>
  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chartSexGlobal"></canvas></div>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chartSexRatio"></canvas></div>
  </div>
  <p>This disparity is not uniform. In some Caribbean and Latin American nations, male rates exceed 50 per 100,000 while female rates remain in single digits. Conversely, in a small number of countries, female rates approach or occasionally exceed male rates—often in contexts with very low absolute numbers and small populations. The persistence of a 3:1 ratio suggests that gender-targeted prevention strategies, particularly those addressing male-on-male violence and firearm access, remain a global policy priority.</p>
  <div class="chart-wrap tall"><canvas id="chartSexCountries"></canvas></div>
</section>

<!-- Age Group Analysis -->
<section id="age-group-analysis">
  <h2>Age Group Analysis</h2>
  <p>When examining age-specific data (disaggregated by sex), young and middle-aged adults face the highest homicide risk. The median rate for males aged 30–44 is 4.08 per 100,000, followed by males 25–29 at 3.99. For females, the 30–44 group also records the highest median (1.39), though absolute levels are far lower than for males.</p>
  <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chartAge"></canvas></div>
  <p>Several patterns emerge:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Youth vulnerability:</strong> Males aged 15–29 experience a steep escalation in risk, with rates climbing from 1.30 (15–17) to 3.99 (25–29). This aligns with global evidence that young men are disproportionately involved in both victimization and perpetration of lethal violence.</li>
    <li><strong>Child safety:</strong> Children under 14 record the lowest median rates for both sexes, though male children still face higher risk than female children.</li>
    <li><strong>Older adults:</strong> Rates for the 60+ group remain elevated relative to children, particularly for males (2.12), suggesting that elder homicide—often linked to robbery or domestic contexts—should not be overlooked in policy design.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note warning">
    <strong>Data limitation:</strong> Age-group breakdowns are not available for all countries or years. The medians above are computed only from observations where age-specific data were reported, and coverage is sparser than for overall rates.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Notable Trends -->
<section id="notable-trends">
  <h2>Notable Country-Level Trends & Outliers</h2>
  <p>Among countries with at least five observations spanning a decade or more, several stand out for dramatic change.</p>
  <h3>Largest Improvements</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Period</th><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Change</th><th>% Change</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>'''
