  </div>
</section>

<!-- Data Limitations & Recommendations -->
<section id="data-limitations-recommendations">
  <h2>Data Limitations & Policy Recommendations</h2>
  <h3>Limitations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> The set of reporting countries changes every year. A decline in the global median may partly reflect the addition of lower-rate countries to the sample rather than uniform safety improvements.</li>
    <li><strong>Coverage gaps:</strong> Only 42% of countries reported data in 2023, down from 79% in 2010. Sub-Saharan Africa and parts of Asia are particularly underrepresented.</li>
    <li><strong>Territorial reporting:</strong> Some territories are listed separately (e.g., Hong Kong, Puerto Rico), which can create confusion in regional aggregates.</li>
    <li><strong>UK caveat:</strong> Data for the United Kingdom refer to England and Wales only.</li>
    <li><strong>No imputation:</strong> Missing years were not interpolated. Any trend line connects observed points and should not be interpreted as continuous coverage.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Targeted male violence prevention:</strong> With a consistent 3:1 male-to-female victimization ratio, policies addressing firearm control, gang diversion, and conflict mediation should prioritize young men in high-rate regions.</li>
    <li><strong>Caribbean and Central American focus:</strong> These regions record the highest median and mean rates. International cooperation on transnational crime, witness protection, and judicial capacity building is urgently needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Youth-specific interventions:</strong> The sharp rise in male homicide risk during ages 15–29 points to the value of school-based violence prevention, vocational training, and community policing.</li>
    <li><strong>Strengthen data infrastructure:</strong> The post-2020 collapse in reporting coverage jeopardizes evidence-based policymaking. Donor governments and UNODC should invest in national statistical and criminal-justice data systems, especially in underrepresented regions.</li>
    <li><strong>Monitor outliers closely:</strong> Countries with rapid deteriorations (e.g., Ecuador, Haiti in recent years) require early-warning mechanisms and rapid-response peacebuilding support before violence becomes entrenched.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<footer>
  <p>Report generated from UNODC Crime Trends Survey data (1990–2023) via Kaggle source. All statistics derived directly from the provided dataset. No external sources or imputed values were used.</p>
</footer>

